export const blocks=[
{id:'1.1',chapter:1,title:'What an AEMT actually is',learner_title:'What the letters mean',
 objectives:['obj-1.1.a'],est_minutes:7,
 cold_open:{text:'Your first shift. The crew chief says the truck is running "AEMT tonight, no medic". Your partner is an EMT. Somewhere between what the two of you can do and what a paramedic could do, there is a line, and by the end of the shift you will have stood on it at least once.',
  decision_point:'Where is that line?'},
 pretest_item:'itm-1.1.001',
 screens:[
  {id:'scr-1.1.1',body_md:'**Four levels, and each one contains the one below it.** Emergency Medical Responder, EMT, Advanced EMT, Paramedic.\n\nA paramedic has not stopped being able to do EMT things. The scopes are cumulative, which is why a mixed crew works and why the highest level present sets the ceiling for the call.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.1.2',body_md:'**EMR** is designed for someone who arrives before an ambulance: compressions, an AED, bleeding control, an airway held open. Minimal equipment, immediate actions.\n\n**EMT** adds a fuller assessment, oxygen, a small set of medications mostly by simple routes, splinting and extrication.\n\nWhat the EMT level does not include is anything that enters a vein or breaks the skin, and that single boundary is where your own new scope begins.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.1.3',body_md:'**AEMT is where access begins.** The defining additions are intravenous access, a wider set of medications and routes, and advanced airway adjuncts short of intubation.\n\nThe pattern is worth naming: AEMT adds *access and a limited drug set*. It does not add independent diagnosis, and it does not add invasive procedures.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.1.4',body_md:'**Paramedic** adds rhythm interpretation and manual defibrillation, intubation, needle decompression, and a much larger formulary.\n\nThe clinical consequence for you is simple: when a patient needs one of those, the useful question is not whether you could attempt it, but how quickly someone who is licensed for it can get there.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.1.5',body_md:'**Nobody on scene can move that line.** Not a paramedic partner, not a physician on the radio, not the urgency of the patient.\n\nThat sounds obvious written down. It is much less obvious at 0300 with a sick patient and a colleague telling you to get on with it — which is why it is worth deciding now, calmly, what you will say.',figure:null}],
 inline_checks:['itm-1.1.003','itm-1.1.006'],
 quiz:['itm-1.1.002','itm-1.1.004','itm-1.1.005','itm-1.1.007','itm-1.1.008'],
 callback_md:'The line runs between access and interpretation. You can start an IV and give what your protocol authorises through it; you cannot interpret a rhythm and shock on your own reading, and you cannot intubate.\n\n**And the line does not move.** Not for a partner, not for a physician, not for how sick the patient is. That last part is the whole of the answer.',
 local_overlay:'ks-amr-kc',version:'1.0.0'},

{id:'1.2',chapter:1,title:'Scope, protocol, licensure',learner_title:'Four words people use wrongly',
 objectives:['obj-1.2.a'],est_minutes:8,
 cold_open:{text:'A colleague at another service tells you they give a drug you have never been allowed to give. Same certification, same state, same national standard. Either one of you is doing something wrong, or the words you are both using do not mean quite what you think they mean.',
  decision_point:'Which is it?'},
 pretest_item:'itm-1.2.001',
 screens:[
  {id:'scr-1.2.1',body_md:'**Scope of practice is a legal ceiling**, set by your state. It is the complete list of things someone at your level may ever do there.\n\nIt is a ceiling and not a checklist. Being within scope means you *may*, not that you *should*, and not that you are authorised today.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.2.2',body_md:'**Protocol is your medical director’s written instruction**, and it sits inside the scope.\n\nA protocol can be narrower than your scope and frequently is. It cannot be wider: no medical director can authorise a skill your licence does not include.\n\nYou practise in the overlap of the two, and the overlap is always the smaller circle.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.2.3',body_md:'**Three words get confused constantly.**\n\n**Certification** says a national body examined you and you passed.\n**Licensure** says a state permits you to practise there.\n**Credentialing** says one employer permits you to practise for them.\n\nCertification travels with you. The other two do not. That is why moving states means starting two of the three again.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.2.4',body_md:'**Standard of care is none of these.** It is how your conduct is judged afterwards: what a reasonable provider of the same level would have done, in the same circumstances.\n\nNot the best provider. Not with hindsight. Protocol is strong evidence of what reasonable looks like, which is why departing from it needs a reason you can state out loud.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.2.5',body_md:'**This is the block worth knowing cold.** Scope confusion is the most common root of disciplinary action at this level, and the reason is structural: nothing on scene stops you.\n\nNo alarm sounds when you cross the line. It is found afterwards, in a review, by someone reading your chart — and by then the act is already done.',figure:null}],
 inline_checks:['itm-1.2.003','itm-1.2.005'],
 quiz:['itm-1.2.002','itm-1.2.004','itm-1.2.006','itm-1.2.007','itm-1.2.008','itm-1.2.009'],
 callback_md:'Neither of you is necessarily wrong. **Scope is set by the state; protocol is set by your medical director inside it.** Two services in one state can hold identical scope and different protocols, and both be practising correctly.\n\nWhat you cannot conclude is that because they may, you may. Your authority comes from your protocol, not from their practice.\n\n*Kansas AEMT scope and the AMR KC standing orders belong here as a local layer. They have not been added to this module yet.*',
 local_overlay:'ks-amr-kc',version:'1.0.0'},

{id:'1.3',chapter:1,title:'How the system is built',learner_title:'What happens before you arrive',
 objectives:['obj-1.3.a'],est_minutes:7,
 cold_open:{text:'By the time you pull up, a neighbour has already called, a dispatcher has already triaged the call and talked someone through compressions, and a first responder has already put hands on the chest. Three things have gone right before you have done anything at all.',
  decision_point:'What else is holding this call up that you cannot see?'},
 pretest_item:'itm-1.3.005',
 screens:[
  {id:'scr-1.3.1',body_md:'**A system is what exists between calls.** Any single call runs through it: someone recognises an emergency and calls, dispatch triages and sends, a crew assesses and treats, the patient is transported, care is handed over.\n\nEvery one of those is a link, and the chain is only as good as its weakest.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.3.2',body_md:'**Public access and dispatch do more than send a truck.** Emergency medical dispatch triages the call, sends the right resource, and gives pre-arrival instructions.\n\nBystander compressions and early bleeding control started on the phone are among the highest-value interventions in the entire system — higher than most of what happens after you arrive.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.3.3',body_md:'**Medical direction is present on every call**, whether or not you speak to anybody. Your protocols are a physician’s judgement, written down in advance.\n\n**Transport and destination** are part of the system too. Which facilities exist, what they can do, and where a patient is taken were decided long before your shift started.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.3.4',body_md:'**Two components look backward rather than forward.**\n\n**Continuous quality improvement** reviews what was done and feeds it into what is taught next. **Public education and prevention** reduces the number of calls that happen at all.\n\nNeither helps the patient in front of you. Both decide what the next hundred patients get.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.3.5',body_md:'**Why this matters on a bad call:** when something is not going to plan, the useful question is which part of the system owns the problem.\n\nNo crew available is a deployment question. No capable hospital within reach is a system-design question. Neither is solved by the two of you driving faster, and both are solved by someone — if they are told.',figure:null}],
 inline_checks:['itm-1.3.002','itm-1.3.006'],
 quiz:['itm-1.3.001','itm-1.3.003','itm-1.3.004','itm-1.3.007','itm-1.3.008'],
 callback_md:'Medical direction, written before you arrived. A destination plan that decided which hospitals could take this patient. A deployment model that put your truck where it was. A CQI process that will look at this call next month.\n\nNone of it is visible on scene, and all of it is holding the call up. **When a call is failing for reasons you cannot fix with your hands, it is usually one of these — and they change only if someone says so.**',
 local_overlay:'ks-amr-kc',version:'1.0.0'},

{id:'1.4',chapter:1,title:'Medical direction',learner_title:'Whose orders you are on',
 objectives:['obj-1.4.a'],est_minutes:8,
 cold_open:{text:'Wheezing, tight, frightened, and your protocol has a standing order for a nebulised bronchodilator that fits this patient exactly. Your partner reaches for the radio to ask medical control for permission. The drug is in your hand.',
  decision_point:'Do you wait for the radio?'},
 pretest_item:'itm-1.4.001',
 screens:[
  {id:'scr-1.4.1',body_md:'**Every prehospital intervention happens under a physician’s authority.** Yours comes in two forms.\n\n**Offline** direction is written in advance: your protocols and standing orders.\n**Online** direction is contemporaneous contact with a physician about this patient, now.\n\nMost of a shift runs on offline direction, and you may never use the other.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.4.2',body_md:'**A standing order is a decision already made.** The physician considered this presentation in advance and wrote down what to do.\n\nWhen the presentation matches, you act. Calling to ask permission for something already permitted spends exactly the minutes the standing order was written to save, and returns no new information.\n\nThe physician who wrote it judged that the delay of a call would cost the patient more than it gained. Waiting anyway quietly overrides that judgement, without anyone deciding to.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.4.3',body_md:'**Call when the written direction runs out.** Three occasions:\n\nThe presentation fits no standing order you hold. You believe a departure from protocol is in the patient’s interest. Or the protocol itself tells you to call.\n\nThat is the whole test: *does written direction already cover this, exactly?* If yes, act. If no, call.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.4.4',body_md:'**An order you cannot lawfully perform is still an order you decline.** A physician cannot extend your licence, any more than a paramedic partner can.\n\nSay what you cannot do and offer what you can. It is uncomfortable once and defensible forever, and it is rare enough that it is worth rehearsing before it happens.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.4.5',body_md:'**Read every radio order back.** Repeat it, have it confirmed, then act.\n\nA misheard drug or a misheard dose is caught at exactly one moment, and that moment is before you give it. The read-back costs about four seconds and it is the only step in the chain that catches the error.\n\nIt also puts the order in your partner’s hearing, which gives a wrong dose a second person who might notice it.',figure:null}],
 inline_checks:['itm-1.4.002','itm-1.4.008'],
 quiz:['itm-1.4.003','itm-1.4.004','itm-1.4.005','itm-1.4.006','itm-1.4.007','itm-1.4.009'],
 callback_md:'**No.** The standing order is medical direction, already given, for exactly this presentation. Give the drug.\n\nThe radio is for when the written direction runs out — when nothing covers the patient, when you want to depart from what does, or when the protocol tells you to call. None of those is true here, and the patient is short of breath while you wait.',
 local_overlay:'ks-amr-kc',version:'1.0.0'},

{id:'1.5',chapter:1,title:'Where the patient goes',learner_title:'Choosing a destination',
 objectives:['obj-1.5.a'],est_minutes:8,
 cold_open:{text:'Two hospitals. One is twenty minutes away and can admit almost anything. The other is fifty minutes away and is the only one within reach that can do the procedure your patient needs tonight. Your patient is stable enough to make the journey, and does not much want to.',
  decision_point:'Which way do you turn out of the driveway?'},
 pretest_item:'itm-1.5.001',
 screens:[
  {id:'scr-1.5.1',body_md:'**Nearest is a default, not a rule.** It is the right answer when the patient will not survive the extra distance, and only then.\n\nThe real question is never which door is closest. It is which route reaches the *definitive treatment* soonest — and for some conditions the nearest door cannot provide it at all.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.5.2',body_md:'**Specialty systems exist because some treatments are time-dependent and not universal.** Trauma centres, stroke centres, cardiac and PCI centres, burn and paediatric centres.\n\nTaking a patient to a facility that must then stabilise and transfer them costs two journeys and the wait in between, which almost always exceeds the longer first transport.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.5.3',body_md:'**Interfacility transfers are destination decisions made by someone else.** The sending clinician specifies the level of care the patient needs in transit, and the service must meet it.\n\nAccepting a transfer above your level is a scope problem that starts before the wheels move, and it is much harder to fix once you are on the road.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.5.4',body_md:'**Patient preference is real and it has limits.** Continuity of care at a hospital that holds their records is a genuine clinical benefit.\n\nThe tighter the clinical time pressure, the less room there is. In a stable patient there is usually room; in a time-critical one there is often none, and saying so plainly is better than deciding silently.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.5.5',body_md:'**Where protocol directs a destination, it governs.** Most disputes dissolve once someone explains the reason rather than asserting the rule.\n\nThe ones that do not are what medical control is for. A contested destination is a legitimate reason to pick up the radio.\n\nName the protocol, name the capability the patient needs, and offer the radio. That sequence settles most disputes before anybody has to be overruled.',figure:null}],
 inline_checks:['itm-1.5.003','itm-1.5.005'],
 quiz:['itm-1.5.002','itm-1.5.004','itm-1.5.006','itm-1.5.007','itm-1.5.008'],
 callback_md:'Toward the procedure — assuming your protocol directs it and he remains stable enough for the journey. The nearer hospital would have to stabilise and transfer him anyway, and that costs him more time than the extra thirty minutes does.\n\n**But tell him why.** A patient who understands that the closer hospital cannot do the thing he needs is usually no longer arguing, and a patient who is simply overruled complains later — reasonably.',
 local_overlay:'ks-amr-kc',version:'1.0.0'},

{id:'1.6',chapter:1,title:'Quality, safety and evidence',learner_title:'How practice changes',
 objectives:['obj-1.6.a'],est_minutes:7,
 cold_open:{text:'An instructor tells you a technique is standard because it has been taught that way for thirty years. Later the same week you read that a large review found no evidence it helps, and some evidence it harms. Both statements are true at once.',
  decision_point:'Which one should change what you do?'},
 pretest_item:'itm-1.6.002',
 screens:[
  {id:'scr-1.6.1',body_md:'**Continuous quality improvement looks for causes, not culprits.** It reviews care across many calls to find the pattern behind a problem, and then changes the system.\n\nA review process people are afraid of stops being told things. Once that happens it is worse than having none, because it produces reassuring reports about a service that is not actually safe.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.6.2',body_md:'**The near miss is the cheapest information a system ever gets.** An error that harmed nobody tells you a hole exists, at no cost to a patient.\n\nReport it. The alternative is waiting for the same hole to be found by someone who is harmed by it, which is how most systems actually learn and is a terrible way to do it.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.6.3',body_md:'**Longevity is not evidence.** A practice taught for thirty years has been taught for thirty years. That is all it establishes.\n\nSeveral long-standing EMS practices have turned out to lack evidence of benefit, and a few carried harm. They persisted because they were taught, not because they worked.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.6.4',body_md:'**Three questions separate an improvement from a fashion.**\n\nWhat evidence supports it, and how strong is it? What outcome was actually measured — survival, or a number on a monitor? And who benefits if this is adopted?\n\nThe third is not cynicism. It is simply worth knowing when a product is involved.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.6.5',body_md:'**You will meet this again in this series.** Two practices ahead of you are taught by the textbook and not supported by current evidence, and the module will show you the book position and the evidence side by side.\n\nThat is deliberate. Being able to say "here is what the book says, here is what the evidence says" is itself part of practising at this level.',figure:null}],
 inline_checks:['itm-1.6.006','itm-1.6.007'],
 quiz:['itm-1.6.001','itm-1.6.003','itm-1.6.004','itm-1.6.005','itm-1.6.008'],
 callback_md:'The review should — but through your medical director, not by you unilaterally on the next call. Protocol is theirs, and appraising evidence against current practice is exactly what that role is for.\n\n**What the thirty years establishes is that it has been taught for thirty years.** Nothing more. Holding both facts at once, without resolving them by deferring to whoever spoke last, is the skill this block is teaching.',
 local_overlay:'ks-amr-kc',version:'1.0.0'},

{id:'1.7',chapter:1,title:'Professionalism and MIH',learner_title:'Conduct, and the wider job',
 objectives:['obj-1.7.a'],est_minutes:6,
 cold_open:{text:'Same address, fourth time this month. Nothing is ever badly wrong. Your partner has stopped bothering to take a full history, and the patient has stopped expecting one. Everyone in the room has quietly agreed this call does not really count.',
  decision_point:'What has gone wrong here, and whose problem is it?'},
 pretest_item:'itm-1.7.001',
 screens:[
  {id:'scr-1.7.1',body_md:'**Professional attributes are not decoration.** Integrity, respect, self-motivation, appearance and manner, and careful communication appear in every professional standard, and each has a clinical reason.\n\nIntegrity is why the record can be relied on. Respect is why the patient tells you things. Self-motivation is why a rarely used skill still works when you need it.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.7.2',body_md:'**Trust is an assessment tool.** History is the larger part of your assessment, and it depends entirely on being told the truth.\n\nA frightened or unconvinced patient tells you less, so you decide with less. That is why manner is treated as clinical rather than cosmetic — it changes the quality of your data.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.7.3',body_md:'**Skills decay whether or not anyone checks.** The ones you rarely use decay fastest, and pressure is exactly when that shows.\n\nSaying "I have not done this in a year and I want to refresh it" is the professional act. Reading a procedure for the first time at a bedside is not competence, and everyone present can tell.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.7.4',body_md:'**Mobile integrated healthcare treats EMS as a healthcare resource that happens to be mobile.** Follow-up visits, assessment at home, help navigating to the service that actually meets the need.\n\nMeasured by needs met rather than patients moved, and often the answer to a person who keeps calling because nothing else has worked.\n\nIt is not a scope change and not a new certification. It is the same providers, sent against a different question.',figure:null},
  {id:'scr-1.7.5',body_md:'**A frequent caller is usually a signal.** Repeated use of an emergency service is a healthcare problem with a healthcare answer, not a character defect and not something to be endured.\n\nIt is also the clearest example of why 1.3 mattered: the fix is almost never on the truck, and the people who can fix it only find out if somebody tells them.',figure:null}],
 inline_checks:['itm-1.7.003','itm-1.7.006'],
 quiz:['itm-1.7.002','itm-1.7.004','itm-1.7.005','itm-1.7.007','itm-1.7.008'],
 callback_md:'The assessment has quietly stopped happening, which means the day something *is* badly wrong, nobody will notice. Familiarity has become a clinical risk.\n\n**And the repeat calls themselves are the signal.** Four visits in a month is a need being met nowhere else — which is a system problem, with a system answer, that will keep recurring until somebody names it.',
 local_overlay:'ks-amr-kc',version:'1.0.0'},

{id:'1.INT',chapter:1,title:'Integration — scope, direction and destination',learner_title:'One call, all three',
 objectives:['obj-1.INT.a'],est_minutes:9,
 cold_open:{text:'Everything in this chapter arrives on the same call, usually in the wrong order and usually at night. Scope, medical direction and destination do not take turns. They land together, and each one changes what the others allow you to do.',
  decision_point:'Can you hold all three at once?'},
 pretest_item:null,
 screens:[
  {id:'scr-1.INT.1',body_md:'**Three questions, running at the same time.**\n\nWhat am I licensed and authorised to do? Who is directing this care, and does the written direction already cover it? And where does this patient need to end up?\n\nWhat follows is one call. Each question comes up more than once, and at least one of the answers changes partway through.',figure:null}],
 inline_checks:[],
 quiz:['itm-1.INT.000'],
 callback_md:'Aspirin on the standing order, without calling. The PCI centre, because the nearer hospital cannot do the thing he needs. A diversion when his airway failed, because capability stops winning the moment the patient will not survive the distance. And a declined order, because nobody on a radio can widen your licence.\n\nFour decisions, all defensible, and the reasons are different in each case. **That is what holding all three at once actually looks like.**',
 local_overlay:'ks-amr-kc',version:'1.0.0'}
];
